80

Having abandoned everything, liberated through the destruction of craving,
whom would one point to, having known it oneself?

354. The gift of the Dhamma surpasses all gifts;
the taste of the Dhamma surpasses all tastes;
the delight in the Dhamma surpasses all delights;
the destruction of craving conquers all suffering.

355. Wealth destroys the fool who does not seek the further shore;
through craving for wealth, the fool destroys others as well as himself.

356. Fields are ruined by weeds; this people is ruined by passion;
therefore what is given to those free from passion yields great fruit.

357. Fields are ruined by weeds; this people is ruined by hatred;
therefore what is given to those free from hatred yields great fruit.

358. Fields are ruined by weeds; this people is ruined by delusion;
therefore what is given to those free from delusion yields great fruit.

359. Fields are ruined by weeds; this people is ruined by longing;
therefore what is given to those free from longing yields great fruit.

The twenty-fourth chapter, on Craving, is concluded.

25. The Chapter on Monks.

360. Restraint of the eye is good; good is restraint of the ear;
restraint of the nose is good; good is restraint of the tongue.

361. Restraint of the body is good; good is restraint of speech;
restraint of the mind is good; good is restraint in all things;
the monk restrained in all things is freed from all suffering.

362. One restrained in hands, restrained in feet,
restrained in speech, supremely restrained,
inwardly delighted, composed,
solitary and content — him they call a monk.

